ukrainian president vladimir zelansky  says his country's forces have liberated  8 000 square kilometers in a lightning  counter-offensive in his country's  northeast but as ukrainian troops raised  the flag in towns and villages in the  hakeem region reports are emerging of  the torture and murder of civilians by  russian forces we'll have more on that  in a moment from our correspondent in  the city of  first some of the scenes from the past  24 hours  a mother reunited with her son  he had been fighting in the ukrainian  army for days in the kharkiv region  until his unit liberated his home  village  my son i have been waiting for you  i have kept all your things everything  they said there are mines in the  apartment  mom you're looking good as always i  missed you so much  ukrainian soldiers follow a trail of  destruction as they press their attack  the russians are in a headlong retreat  shedding their equipment as they fall  back  they were running like mice they were  running and abandoning everything  vehicles their own man  they even abandoned one of their own who  was wounded just to get away  the kremlin denies reports that the  military setbacks are turning people  against president vladimir putin  russians support the president and the  mood of the people confirms this  the people stand firmly behind the  decisions made by the head of state  but as ukraine continues to roll back  gains made by russian forces early in  the war  putin's decision to invade ukraine  could come back to haunt him  and we can join dw correspondent  emmanuel shaz in ukraine's second  largest city khaki welcome emanuel um  let us start with these uh reports of uh  torture in the hakiv region what have  you been hearing  well uh phil you might hear behind me  there's a national anthem being played  just as every day at 9 00 a.m ukraine  time in memory of all of the people  civilians and  soldiers alike who fell  during this war they are called the  heroes of  ukraine and it  tells you just how how uh you know  important it is for people to know what  happened  in those occupied territories to give  you an idea after a month of occupation  in bucharest we've seen the horrors  unfolding the war crimes and this is  what we might be looking at in the  places that have been  freed that have been liberated over the  past days it's over 300 settlements  which have been  which have been liberated and from then  start to emerge very very grim reports  of people who have been arrested who  have been tortured  some people  apparently also have been killed there's  a lot of pictures circulating on social  media  of you know corpses being uncovered by  soldiers as they liberate the town so to  give you an idea the general prosecutor  of ukraine already investigates uh over  32  000 cases of war crimes of crimes  against  humanity committed by the russian army  and it seems as if we're looking at more  cases being uncovered every day here in  the harakeev region  and what have people been telling you as  the tide of this war appears to be  turning in ukraine's favor  well what really um  what really came true since i uh i've  been here in ukraine is the resilience  of people here they really have faith in  their army but even then uh i think even  ukrainians were surprised at how  this counter-offensive uh was successful  they expected a counter offensive for  months but they  had no means to evaluate its successes  and it went you know  above and beyond what they could have  hoped uh for example you know uh they  the key cities here in the harkief  region such as uh eastern  such as copeans who are very very  important for the russian army have been  liberated and  in doing so this really hinders  russian  ways of of getting more ammunitions more  material conveyed to those soldiers of  them who are still on the front line and  it seems really that russia russia  really struggles to keep  its defenses on that new front line  and president zielenski said in his  evening address on tuesday that he hopes  for a rapid return to normality in  liberated areas what has that looked  like in places like butcher that have  already been freed from the russian  presence  well uh you know the road to normality  uh will will be long first off because  most of the places uh here have also  been shelled here in the city of harkief  we haven't seen a fighting per se but  there's daily uh shelling like day in  day out and especially  at night  buildings are being shelled so this will  be about uh rebuilding the country this  will take  years of course but people here are very  much ready to do so  thank you for that emmanuel shaz in  hakim  german chancellor olaf schultz has  repeated his call for vladimir putin to  withdraw his troops from ukraine and a  phone call with the russian president  chancellor schultz urged him to find a  diplomatic solution based on a ceasefire  as a offensive by keeves forces made  speedy progress a german government  spokesperson said mr schultz also urged  mr putin to respect ukraine's  sovereignty and territorial integrity  dw political correspondent hans brandt  can tell us more welcome hans what sort  of diplomatic solution is germany  calling for what would that look like  well there's those first three steps  that were just mentioned the  ceasefire would be the first step the  second step that has been demanded is  that russia withdraw its truth from  ukrainian territory the third step would  be for russia simply to respect  ukrainian territorial integrity  but in fact all of these are just  preconditions for diplomatic solutions  for diplomatic  negotiations um  the the exact framework or the exact uh  look of such a solution is not really  clear at the moment  even the german government has not  really been given any details about what  it might think about in ukraine at the  same time there have been discussions  with western allies about what kind of  security guarantees ukraine might need  after the war um  equipping the ukrainian army military  support and support from western uh  countries this in fact has then uh  called for a very severe reaction from  dmitry medieva for the former prime  minister and president of russia who  said that would be tantamount to ukraine  joining nato so we're very far away from  a diplomatic solution at the moment okay  and so  let's look at what  germany has been doing because key has  been consistent in its demands for  western support send us more weapons  particularly tanks but this seems to be  a line the german chancellor is  reluctant to cross and this appears to  be causing friction within his  government  yes indeed the the major dispute is  around tanks and armored personnel  carriers these are demanded by kiev  german tanks leopard tanks  and they they are basically available  but the german government has been  saying repeatedly in the last couple of  days that is not going to go alone in  making such a decision  the defense minister said recently that  no western country has so far supplied  western technology tanks to ukraine and  germany would not be the first to do  that but within the german government  there are voices that are saying that  with the initiative on the side of  ukraine at the moment on the battlefield  this should be taken  as  the time to supply tanks so that ukraine  can make further advances in this battle  against the russian invasion okay thank  you hans dw political correspondent hans  brandt
 

